      "title": "Iran and Science Olympiads | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Iran’s mathematics olympiad record, the meaning of competition rankings, and its role as host of the 2017 informatics olympiad."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "competition-scope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A competition record, not a school-system score",
        "content": "International science olympiads make individual achievement visible through common tasks and published results. The International Mathematical Olympiad, or IMO, is a pre-university mathematics competition. Its participants represent national delegations, but contestants solve the problems individually. The competition’s official explanation describes teams of up to six students who must be under twenty and not enrolled in post-secondary education. [s1]\n\nThese boundaries are central to interpretation. Results describe selected competitors under examination conditions. They do not measure average attainment, educational equity, or the research productivity of future graduates. A country can have outstanding contestants without that result establishing how opportunities are distributed among its schools.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iran-record",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Iran’s mathematics record",
        "content": "Iran first participated in the IMO in 1985. Its country results include first place in 1998, demonstrating that its record extends well beyond recent competitions. [s2][s3]\n\nThe annual series below preserves the fifteen editions from 2012 through 2026. Iran’s published rank fluctuates substantially: fifth in 2017, twenty-ninth in 2021, eighth in 2022, and fourteenth in 2026. These positions should be read as relative results within each edition. They are not a smooth measure of underlying educational progress, and the distance between adjacent ranks is not a fixed difference in mathematical ability. [s3]\n\nA lower rank number represents a better placement. Raw scores are deliberately excluded from this trend chart because different editions use different problems; equal points across years need not represent equivalent difficulty.",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "imo-rank-chart",
        "type": "chart",
        "content": {
          "type": "line",
          "title": "Iran’s published IMO team rank, 2012–2026",
          "dataset_id": "iran-imo-rank",
          "x_field": "period_label",
          "series_ids": ["team-rank"],
          "x_axis_label": "Competition year",
          "y_axis_label": "Published rank; lower is better",
          "y_axis_min": 0,
          "show_legend": false,
          "connect_nulls": false,
          "interpretation": "Iran placed within the top ten in five of these fifteen editions. The results fluctuate rather than showing continuous improvement; ranks are relative to each edition’s participating teams."
        },
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "contest-process",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "How the IMO examination is organized",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"National selection programme\"] -->|\"Selects contestants\"| B[\"National delegation\"]\n  C[\"Participating countries\"] -->|\"Submit proposed problems\"| D[\"Problem selection committee\"]\n  D -->|\"Prepares shortlist\"| E[\"IMO jury\"]\n  E -->|\"Chooses examination problems\"| F[\"Individual examinations\"]\n  B -->|\"Contestants sit examinations\"| F\n```",
        "summary": "This diagram describes the international competition framework. It does not establish the current stages or administrative rules of Iran’s domestic selection programme.",
        "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "beyond-mathematics",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Participation beyond mathematics",
        "content": "Iran also hosted the International Olympiad in Informatics, a separate competition in computing, in Tehran in 2017. The official archive dates the event from 28 July to 4 August and explicitly records an independently organized offsite competition in Russia. That qualification matters: an event’s headline contestant total should not automatically be described as attendance physically in Tehran. [s5]\n\nHosting and winning are distinct forms of involvement. Hosting establishes an organizational role; medals establish individual examination outcomes. Neither alone proves later scientific discovery or commercial success. A fuller account of Iranian science olympiads requires comparable evidence from other subjects and verified documentation of domestic selection, training, and access.",
        "source_ids": ["s5"]
      }
    ],
