    "description": "Iranian scientific publications form part of an international scholarly record whose measured size depends on database coverage and counting methods. UNESCO documents historical growth, while newer indicators require careful separation from that earlier series.",
    "tags": ["Scientific publications", "Bibliometrics", "Research", "Scopus", "UNESCO"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T17:52:59Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T17:52:59Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iranian Scientific Publications and Research Trends | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Read Iran’s historical publication series and understand why database coverage, citations, collaboration, and reporting periods matter."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "publication-record",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What publication statistics describe",
        "content": "Publication statistics make part of Iran’s research activity visible internationally, but their meaning depends on the underlying database. UNESCO’s 2021 science report presents a historical series drawn from Scopus and processed by Science-Metrix. Its stated coverage excludes arts, humanities, and social sciences. It should therefore be read as an indicator of the covered scientific literature, rather than a census of all Iranian scholarship. [s1]\n\nThe series below shows a substantial expansion over its documented period, with a small interruption in 2015. It is retained as a historical series rather than extended using figures from another provider. A later number bearing the same broad label could use different document types, subject boundaries, or counting rules. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "publication-chart",
        "type": "chart",
        "content": {
          "type": "line",
          "title": "Iranian scientific publication volume in UNESCO’s historical series, 2012–2019",
          "dataset_id": "unesco-publications",
          "x_field": "period_label",
          "series_ids": ["publication-count"],
          "x_axis_label": "Publication year",
          "y_axis_label": "Publications; unscaled count",
          "y_axis_min": 0,
          "show_legend": false,
          "connect_nulls": false,
          "interpretation": "The reported count rose from 34,889 in 2012 to 60,562 in 2019. Growth was not uninterrupted: the 2015 count was slightly below 2014. These historical values do not establish the current publication level."
        },
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "newer-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Newer evidence without a false continuation",
        "content": "WIPO’s 2025 Global Innovation Tracker reports a 3% decline in Iran’s scientific publications between 2023 and 2024. This provides a newer directional observation, but the inspected profile does not supply the underlying annual counts needed to continue the UNESCO chart. The two indicators are consequently kept separate. [s2]\n\nA reporting year also differs from a publication’s release year. A profile issued in 2025 can describe research published in 2024, and different indicators in that same profile can refer to different periods. WIPO explicitly notes that its tracker uses the latest available observations and that time windows can differ when data are missing. Reading every figure as a measurement of the report’s title year would create a misleading timeline. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-impact",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Volume, visibility, and impact",
        "content": "The Nature Index illustrates a different measurement choice. Its country profile distinguishes an absolute article Count from a fractional Share of author contribution and restricts coverage to a selected journal set. Its figures therefore answer a narrower question than a broad publication database. A rolling twelve-month window must also be distinguished from a calendar year. [s3]\n\nThese differences affect interpretation before any ranking is considered. A reader comparing countries or years should first ask which journals and subjects are included, how collaborative papers are credited, and whether the periods align. Publication volume, authorship contribution, and scientific influence should not be treated as identical outcomes.",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "working-conditions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The working conditions behind the record",
        "content": "Publication totals alone do not explain the conditions under which research is produced. A February 2026 Nature correspondence by Mohammad Hosseini identifies sanctions, domestic political pressures, financial obstacles, unreliable infrastructure, and barriers to international exchange. His account offers contemporary context, but it does not quantify their separate effects on publication output. [s4]\n\nThe defensible conclusion is narrower than either celebration or dismissal: the inspected evidence documents substantial historical publishing activity, while a complete contemporary assessment still requires a recent annual series with explicit counting rules and evidence on impact, disciplinary variation, and research integrity.",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [
      {
        "id": "unesco-publications",
        "title": "Volume of scientific publications in Iran, UNESCO Science Report 2021 series",
        "geography": "Iran, as attributed in UNESCO’s Scopus-based country series",
